  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-127094",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The modes of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ at the global maxima of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maxima of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $0\\le x\\le 3$ is $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2).$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)=0\\,⟹\\,\\pi x=0+k\\pi \\,⟹\\,x=k,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\nAmong these critical points, only four lie within the interval $[0,3].$ These are $x=0,1,2,3,$ so we keep them all.\n\n- The derivative doesn't exist at the endpoints $x=0,3.$ So, these are critical points as well, but they are included above.\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1,2,3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1))+2)=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (2))+2)=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (3))+2)=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest values are\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the modes of $X$ are $0$ and $2.$",
